    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d466">

                <pb facs="0790" n="780" xml:id="pg780"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">General Marshall to Colonel Marshall S. Carter </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-06-29">29 June 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1013. Doctor T. V. Soong has answered by letter reference my 1006<anchor n="14"
                        xml:id="d466-f1"/> and your 92816.<anchor n="15" xml:id="d466-f2"/> He
                    stated that it was his intention that any cash required for the Chinese Air
                    Force program in the United States should come out of the proceeds of the U. S.
                    Army obligations in Chinese National currency, negotiations for which are about
                    to begin in Washington. In connection with his message passed to you in 1012,
                    Doctor Soong wished it understood that when the settlement of the U. S. Army
                    obligation is effected, the 4,000,000 dollars thus advanced will be reimbursed
                    from the proceeds thereof. May 1014<anchor n="16" xml:id="d466-f3"/> outlines my
                    answer to Doctor Soong in this regard. It would appear to be a matter which will
                    have to be watched since the Yuan debt may eventually be over allocated.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
